# Gradient descent

Gradient descent is a local optimizer for smooth functions when you can compute
a gradient. Each step moves opposite the gradient, using a fixed step size or a
line search to choose the distance. It uses little memory and offers a simple
baseline before trying a method that learns more from previous steps.

[API
reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gradient_descent/struct.GradientDescent.html)

  | Property    | Requirement                                                                                    |
  | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  | Objective   | A smooth scalar function                                                                       |
  | Evaluations | `CostFunction` and `Gradient`                                                                  |
  | Search      | Local, from one starting point                                                                 |
  | Constraints | Unconstrained; use [projected gradient descent](../projected-gradient-descent/) for box bounds |
  | Parameters  | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64`                                                   |

## When to use it

Consider gradient descent when you want a straightforward method with little
storage beyond the parameters and gradient. A fixed step is useful when you
already know an appropriate scale for your problem. A line search can help when
a single step size would be difficult to choose.

On a narrow, curved valley, successive gradient directions can zigzag across the
valley while making little progress along it. Compare [nonlinear conjugate
gradient](../nonlinear-cg/) or [L-BFGS](../../quasi-newton/l-bfgs/) when plain
descent takes too many steps. Both use information from earlier iterations to
construct their directions.

Gradient descent follows a local path, so different starting points can lead to
different minima. For gradients computed from samples or minibatches, compare
[SGD](../sgd/), which provides mini-batch updates with seeded sampling.

## How it works

At the current point, the negative gradient is the direction of steepest local
decrease in the Euclidean norm. Gradient descent chooses a step length `alpha`
and updates the parameters with `x_next = x - alpha * gradient`. [Nocedal and
Wright (2006)](#ref-nocedal-wright-2006) describe this approach and the role of
line searches.

`GradientDescent::new(alpha)` uses the same step length at every iteration. You
can instead supply a line search through
`GradientDescent::with_line_search(search)`. For example, the [backtracking line
search](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/backtracking/struct.Backtracking.html)
reduces a trial step until the objective decreases enough to satisfy the Armijo
condition. One solver iteration can then require several objective evaluations.

Optional heavy-ball momentum adds a fraction of the previous displacement to the
next step. Basin uses `velocity_next = beta * velocity - alpha * gradient`, then
adds that velocity to the parameters, starting from zero velocity. This is the
method of [Polyak (1964)](#ref-polyak-1964) when the step length is fixed. The
default momentum coefficient is zero, which gives plain gradient descent.

## Example

This quadratic has its minimum at `[1.0, -2.0]`. The example supplies an
analytic gradient, chooses a fixed step, sets a gradient tolerance on the
solver, and limits iterations through the executor.

<!-- compile: solver-gradient-descent -->
```rust
use basin::{CostFunction, Executor, Gradient, GradientDescent, Termination};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct Quadratic;

impl CostFunction for Quadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + 4.0 * (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Gradient for Quadratic {
    type Gradient = Vec<f64>;

    fn gradient(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<Self::Gradient, Self::Error> {
        Ok(vec![2.0 * (x[0] - 1.0), 8.0 * (x[1] + 2.0)])
    }
}

fn main() {
    let solver =
        GradientDescent::new(0.1).with_absolute_gradient_tolerance(1e-8);

    let result = Executor::from_start(Quadratic, solver, vec![-3.0, 5.0])
        .max_iter(1_000)
        .run()
        .unwrap();

    assert!(matches!(
        result.report.termination,
        Termination::Converged(_)
    ));
    assert!((result.param()[0] - 1.0).abs() < 1e-7);
    assert!((result.param()[1] + 2.0).abs() < 1e-7);
}
```

To choose the step through backtracking instead, construct the solver with
`GradientDescent::with_line_search(basin::Backtracking::new())`.

## Practical considerations

### Choose the step for your scale

A fixed step does not check whether the objective decreases. A step that is too
large can overshoot or diverge, while a small step can make progress slow.
Rescale parameters with very different units, or try a line search when you
cannot choose a useful fixed step.

### Add momentum carefully

`with_momentum(beta)` carries a fraction of the previous velocity into each
update. Coefficients between zero and one can help on elongated valleys, but can
also cause overshoot. Reduce the fixed step when adding momentum. With a line
search, the acceptance condition applies to the trial along the negative
gradient; the additional velocity changes the point Basin actually takes.

## Implementation

Basin publishes the current point, objective, and gradient through
`FirstOrderState`. Fresh initialization evaluates the starting point and resets
the line search and momentum velocity. The solver stores a persistent velocity
vector only when momentum is enabled.

Without momentum, the solver reuses an accepted point's objective and gradient
when the line search provides them. With momentum, it evaluates the actual point
after adding the velocity. A failed line search returns a numerical failure
termination while retaining the previous point.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Custom line
searches can require additional vector operations. See the API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gradient_descent/struct.GradientDescent.html#backends)
for the exact capability requirements.

